<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail Speransky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1772–1839</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail Speransky was a Russian statesman and reformer, the leading adviser of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> until his fall and exile in 1812. As governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> from 1819 to 1821 he drew up the Siberian reforms of 1822, which among other things abolished the khanship of the Middle Juz and placed the Kazakh steppe under Russian district administration. Under Nicholas I he oversaw the codification of Russian law, published in 1830 and 1832.</p></div>
